{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/10/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e45ef808\" lines=\"31-46\">\n<description>\nMark the shallowest and deepest columns of a solid terrain boundary with vertical colored guide lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- The first row is a solid empty separator.\n- Below it, each column consists of upper fill cells followed by a contiguous suffix of terrain cells.\n- The bottom row is all terrain, so every column has a well-defined first terrain row.\n\nRule:\n- Find the column whose first terrain row is latest; it is unique and its upper fill run becomes maroon.\n- Find the rightmost column whose first terrain row is earliest; its upper fill run becomes yellow.\n- Preserve the empty separator, terrain cells, and all other upper fill cells.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is a column-height summary of the terrain profile: color the deepest upper run and the highest terrain peak.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6d1d5c90\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nRemove the left control column and roll the remaining image downward by the red marker's row.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a rectangular grid with a one-cell-wide control column on the left.\n- That control column is all pink except for exactly one red marker pixel.\n- The columns to the right are the image that remains in the output.\n\nRule\n- Delete the left control column.\n- Let r be the row index of the red marker in that column.\n- Cyclically shift the remaining image downward by r rows.\n\nKey insight\n- The red marker's vertical position encodes the row-roll amount; the pink\n  column is only a control strip and is removed after reading it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"230f2e48\" lines=\"19-38\">\n<description>\nFold each straight red rod at its black marker, leaving the gray-anchored side fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground object is a straight horizontal or vertical 4-connected rod.\n- Each rod contains many pixels of one line color, one endpoint marker, and one\n  interior control marker.\n\nRule:\n- For every rod, keep the segment from the endpoint marker through the control\n  marker unchanged.\n- Move the line-color tail on the far side of the control marker to a\n  perpendicular ray starting next to that marker.\n- Choose the perpendicular direction with more space to the grid edge.\n\nKey insight:\n- The endpoint marker identifies the fixed side of the rod, while the control\n  marker is the hinge; the tail folds into the larger open half-plane.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f931b4a8\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nCount the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants.\n- Black is the background.\n- The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions.\n- The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.\n\nRule:\n- Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant.\n- Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant.\n- If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position.\n- If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.\n\nKey insight:\n- The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2b9ef948\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nMove the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is black.\n- One 3x3 yellow frame has a non-yellow control-color center.\n- A second control-color pixel is connected through gray path pixels to a lone yellow endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Use the vector from the second control-color pixel to the lone yellow endpoint as the frame's translation.\n- Fill the whole output with the control color.\n- Draw the translated yellow frame and extend yellow diagonal rays outward from its four corners to the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: The gray path's turns are irrelevant; its two colored endpoints encode the net displacement to apply to the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8fff9e47\" lines=\"8-25\">\n<description>\nTurn a 4-cell-wide strip of 2x2 blocks into four nested corner-fill patterns.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an even-sized rectangle whose shorter dimension is four pixels.\nIt can be partitioned exactly into 2x2 blocks, giving one block for each layer\nof the output quadrants.\n\nRule\nRead the 2x2 blocks in reverse row-major order: bottom-right block first,\nmoving left, then moving upward. For each block, send its top-left,\ntop-right, bottom-left, and bottom-right pixels to the layer sequences of the\nupper-left, upper-right, lower-left, and lower-right output quadrants.\n\nKey insight\nEach input 2x2 block supplies the four colors for one concentric layer, and\nthe layer order is the block order from bottom-right back to top-left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e4d4f7c\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nCompress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all\nforeground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.\n\nRule\nKeep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.\nOutput row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are\nrecolored pink.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated\nrow is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e576d6e\" lines=\"159-181\">\n<description>\nDraw the marker-colored route through the centers of all doors in the parallel wall maze.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background, parallel full-span walls, one three-pixel door\n  segment in each wall, and two marker pixels sharing a color.\n- Walls are either all vertical or all horizontal, and the markers are on\n  opposite outer sides of the wall set.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the scene unchanged except for the route.\n- Visit the door centers in wall order from the upper/left marker side to\n  the lower/right marker side.\n- In each corridor, draw an orthogonal path to the cell just before the next\n  wall, move along that wall to the door center, cross the center, and\n  continue to the next corridor; after the last door, connect to the other\n  marker.\n\nKey insight:\n- The three-pixel door segments mark the only crossing points through a set\n  of parallel walls, so the output is the corridor path threaded through\n  those centers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"da6e95e5\" lines=\"70-81\">\n<description>\nExtract the central object from the marker cluster whose markers have the same color.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background and several clusters; each cluster has four\nsame-color corner marker objects around one central monochromatic object.\nRule\nFind every central object enclosed by four aligned corner markers of one color,\nthen choose the unique one whose object color equals the marker color.\nKey insight\nThe answer is not the largest or most frequent object; it is the center of the\nself-colored cluster, cropped to that object's bounding box with background kept.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"981571dc\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\n\nRule:\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
